{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:14Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"start","detail":"Shift 20260809_122036 begins. Prior shift (20260809_085144) left an OPEN incident inc-001 (product-catalog fail-flag cascade). My job here is fresh detection, not to re-assess that carryover — alert-grouping does that next.","reasoning":"Personality reminds me: judgment is mine every time; evidence is currency; a quiet shift is a complete answer. I read the base playbook (triangulate, blame dependencies not symptoms, severity from user impact, flags as leads not proof) and started with a broad sweep."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:20Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_flags","detail":"GET http://10.10.1.141:4001/list -> 15 flags, all defaultVariant off (except loadGeneratorVUs=5). /status/<flag> for productCatalogFailure, paymentFailure, cartFailure, recommendationCacheFailure, adFailure, adHighCpu, emailMemoryLeak, imageSlowLoad, kafkaQueueProblems, loadGeneratorTraffic all return defaultVariant off.","reasoning":"Static default only. flagd here doesn't publish authoritative live values through /status — same limitation the prior shift documented. Cannot conclude a flag is definitively off from this, but I have no positive signal of any active fault-injection either."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:25Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_metric","detail":"sum by (service_name)(rate(traces_span_metrics_calls_total{status_code=\"STATUS_CODE_ERROR\"}[15m])) — only nonzero: ad=0.00119 err/s, recommendation=0.00119 err/s, flagd=0.00357 err/s. Everything else 0.","reasoning":"Very low error rate. But raw magnitude doesn't tell me anything without the total-call denominator or the specific span identity — I have to actually look at what these spans are before I decide."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:30Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_metric","detail":"1h totals: ad 6.1 err / 367 spans (~1.66%), recommendation 5.08 err / 5.08 spans (100%, tiny sample), cart 0/240, frontend 0/240, product-catalog 0/0, checkout 0/0, payment 0/1. image-provider 0/1075, load-generator 0/3946.","reasoning":"Two things stand out. (1) recommendation showing 100% error is a red flag — I need to see the span. (2) product-catalog/checkout/payment all at exactly zero spans is a change from the prior shift's picture where product-catalog was heavy traffic; either the fault-injection stopped everything from calling product-catalog, or the load-generator scenario changed. Either way, this alone is not evidence of a live incident — 'no traffic' is not the same as 'traffic failing'."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:38Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_metric","detail":"5m-bucket time series for the last hour: ad steady at 30-31.25 total / 1.25 err per 5m bucket (alternating with 0s due to evaluation windows), recommendation steady at 1.25/1.25 per bucket, all others flat. No burst, no ramp, no gaps.","reasoning":"This is not incident dynamics. Incidents have onset, growth, and either taper or persistence. Perfectly flat, alternating-bucket signals scream 'periodic background phenomenon' not 'live failure.' Formed hypothesis: these error spans are a periodic, non-user-facing subscription/heartbeat pattern. Time to try to disprove it."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:45Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"search_traces","detail":"Tempo search resource.service.name=ad && status=error over last hour, limit=5: all 5 results are name='flagd.evaluation.v2.Service/EventStream', each durationMs=600000-600004 (exactly 10 minutes), rootTraceName same. Same shape for recommendation (5/5 results are 10-minute EventStream spans).","reasoning":"Every 'error' span is a flagd EventStream subscription that ran for exactly 10 minutes. gRPC server-streaming RPCs are inherently long-lived; when the client-side deadline hits or the stream is rotated, the span closes with an error status. This is normal client-driven stream renewal, not a real error."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:52Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"disconfirm","detail":"Pulled full trace 6ed189c01b87839a50a0bbda1b185c15: single ad span, kind=SPAN_KIND_CLIENT, rpc.grpc.status_code=4 (DEADLINE_EXCEEDED), rpc.method=EventStream, rpc.service=flagd.evaluation.v2.Service, thread.name=flagd-rpc-retry-scheduler, events show a message stream received every 20 seconds for the whole 10 minutes.","reasoning":"DEADLINE_EXCEEDED on a 10-minute streaming RPC that received a heartbeat every 20s is exactly what a healthy long-poll looks like — the client hits its own configured deadline and reconnects. rpc.retry-scheduler in the thread name confirms this is scheduled reconnect logic, not a failure. Disconfirmation attempt strengthens rather than weakens the 'noise' hypothesis."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:21:58Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"disconfirm","detail":"Tempo search { resource.service.name=\"ad\" && status=error && name!=\"flagd.evaluation.v2.Service/EventStream\" } over last hour: traces=[] (zero results). Broader search { status=error && name!=EventStream } same window: only 2 flagd 'flagSync' spans of ~1-1.7ms.","reasoning":"After removing the EventStream noise, ad has zero error traces. Across the entire system, the only remaining 'errors' are two ~1ms flagd flagSync spans — flagd-internal machinery, no user path involvement. This is decisive: no real failure signal exists."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:22:04Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_metric","detail":"sum by (status,url)(increase(k6_http_reqs_total[1h])): every frontend-proxy URL counter (500 and 200 both) reads 0 for the past hour. Only k6-side counter with mass is flagd:8016/ofrep/v1/evaluate/loadGeneratorTraffic (3931.9 200s — the wrapper's polling to check the master traffic flag).","reasoning":"The load-generator is running (still polling flagd) but its HTTP scenario is not producing requests. Aligns with product-catalog/checkout/payment/cart having zero server spans. There is no synthetic user traffic to fail — so absence of failures at those services is not by itself informative."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:22:10Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_metric","detail":"sum by (http_response_status_code, service_name)(increase(http_server_request_duration_seconds_count[1h])): ad 360 x 200, frontend 60 x 200, all other codes (500,400,404,308,304) = 0.","reasoning":"Both services still handling live HTTP traffic — the ad service is receiving ~360 successful requests/hour (probably keep-alives from the frontend), frontend is serving ~60 requests/hour of internal or probe traffic. Zero 5xx or 4xx. Whatever traffic exists is succeeding."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:22:16Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"query_logs","detail":"count_over_time on all opentelemetry-demo/* services with 'error|exception|fail|panic|fatal' regex over last hour: only match is opentelemetry-demo/ad with 4+3=7 lines total. Sample body: 'Transport failed' with io.grpc.netty ... Http2Exception 'HTTP/2 client preface string missing or corrupt'.","reasoning":"7 error-shaped log lines / hour, all on ad, all describing a malformed HTTP/2 preface — matches container health probes hitting the gRPC port with plain TCP or HTTP/1. Rate (~7/hr) also aligns with the EventStream rotation rate. Application-layer log signal is silent."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:22:22Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"disconfirm","detail":"Tightened window: sum by (service_name)(increase(traces_span_metrics_calls_total{status_code=STATUS_CODE_ERROR}[10m])) — only recommendation 1.11 and flagd 2.22 (EventStream/flagSync). Tempo search excluding EventStream+flagSync in the last ~10 min returns 0 traces.","reasoning":"Ruling out 'the burst is just starting'. Nothing new is emerging inside the freshest window."}
{"ts":"2026-08-09T12:22:28Z","agent":"log-triage","action":"no_alert","detail":"Zero alerts raised this shift.","reasoning":"Everything labeled 'error' in the last hour is either (a) a 10-minute gRPC server-streaming subscription closing with DEADLINE_EXCEEDED on renewal — a healthy client-driven pattern on flagd's EventStream, invariant every 10 minutes for every subscribed service — or (b) a sub-2ms flagd internal flagSync span. No user-path error traces, no HTTP 5xx on any service metric, no application ERROR logs, no k6 client-side failures. Load-generator HTTP scenario is not currently generating scenario requests, so many services (product-catalog, checkout, payment) are simply idle rather than failing. Idle is not an alert. The prior shift's open incident inc-001 concerns product-catalog — its span traffic is zero here, meaning I cannot from log-triage's vantage point say whether it 'recovered' or is simply not being exercised; that's alert-grouping's judgment call, not mine."}

